+# Weigh new services against Postgres
+
+For whoever proposes adding a service to larder. A new service needs to do something Postgres cannot, and two
+volunteers able to restore it; otherwise it stays in Postgres or the one web app. Written after this argument was
+made for the third time in a year ([issue 70](../../notes/issue-70.md)), so the next proposal can start from the rule
+instead of rediscovering it.
+
+## Context
+
+Larder runs one database and one web app on volunteer time; nobody is paid to be on call. Three times a new service
+was proposed to solve a problem Postgres could also solve:
+
+- **2023, Redis for sessions**: turned down before launch, because only one volunteer would have run it. Sessions
+  stayed in Postgres ([keep sessions in the database](0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md)).
+- **March 2025, Redis for Celery's queue**: adopted, then evicted 300 queued receipt jobs one Saturday night with no
+  error logged; the one volunteer who understood Redis spent a day resending receipts by hand. Jobs moved to
+  procrastinate on Postgres ([run background jobs on Postgres](0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md)).
+- **October 2026, Elasticsearch for pantry search**: proposed to fix slow, exact-match-only search; dropped once
+  Postgres full-text search on the item name met the same need at 40 ms.
+
+Each time, the service would have done something Postgres could also do, and only one volunteer could have restored
+it when it broke.
+
+## Decision
+
+Before adding a service, both must hold:
+
+- it does something Postgres (or the existing web app) cannot do, and
+- at least two volunteers can restore it without the person who set it up.
+
+This does not mean never: the card gateway lives outside larder because larder cannot process cards itself, and a
+hosted service that larder's volunteers never have to operate does not add an operational burden the way a
+self-run service does.
+
+## Consequences
+
+A proposal for a new service states up front what it does that Postgres cannot, and names the second volunteer who
+can restore it. A proposal that cannot answer both starts from Postgres instead.
